WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
Lifetimes for security are a practical
design and review discipline.

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:07.206
Start by identifying the owner before
reasoning about a reference.

3
00:00:07.286 --> 00:00:10.428
A reference is valid only because some
owner keeps the data alive.

4
00:00:10.508 --> 00:00:13.821
If the owner cannot outlive the reference,
the design should change.

5
00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.844
Keep borrows narrow and tied to real use.

6
00:00:15.924 --> 00:00:18.323
Avoid holding references across unrelated
work.

7
00:00:18.403 --> 00:00:22.116
Be careful with temporary values, borrowed
fields, collection references,

8
00:00:22.196 --> 00:00:24.595
and data that crosses async or thread
boundaries.

9
00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:28.674
Shorter, clearer lifetimes make code
easier for the compiler and easier for humans

10
00:00:28.754 --> 00:00:29.268
to review.

11
00:00:29.348 --> 00:00:31.747
Use annotations to describe real
relationships,

12
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:34.226
not to force a design that should own data
instead.

13
00:00:34.306 --> 00:00:37.905
If a function cannot return a valid
borrowed reference, return owned data.

14
00:00:37.985 --> 00:00:41.298
If a struct with borrowed fields makes the
API difficult for callers,

15
00:00:41.378 --> 00:00:44.063
consider whether owned or shared data
would be clearer.

16
00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.142
Treat async tasks, threads, unsafe code,

17
00:00:46.222 --> 00:00:48.736
and FFI as areas that need explicit
lifetime review.

18
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:52.815
These boundaries may outlive local scopes
or move beyond what Rust can fully check.

19
00:00:52.895 --> 00:00:55.465
Document safety contracts, test boundary
behavior,

20
00:00:55.545 --> 00:00:58.744
and update lifetime assumptions as APIs
and dependencies evolve.

21
00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:00.766
The main goal is clear trust boundaries.

22
00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:03.817
Lifetimes help Rust teams design code with
valid references,

23
00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:07.381
visible ownership relationships, safe
APIs, and reviewable data flow.

24
00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:11.460
That is why lifetimes matter not only for
compilation, but for secure engineering.
